// The bonepose package: a skeleton as data, a pose of it, forward kinematics
// and two-bone inverse kinematics. A program reaches it with 'import
// bonepose', and gets:
//
// - Skeleton: bones in an order where a parent comes before its child, each
//   with its rest transform relative to its parent (translation and rotation;
//   scale is 1, so a pose is rigid and normals need no special care), and
//   sockets, named attachment points: a parent bone and an offset in its
//   frame (the eyes, a hand that holds a sword). A socket is not a bone: it
//   costs nothing to pose and is found by the same rule, bone times offset.
// - Pose: one local transform per bone, relative to the bone's parent. A pose
//   starts as the skeleton's rest pose; animation changes the rotations (and
//   the root bone's translation).
// - Forward kinematics: 'modelTransforms' composes a pose down the tree into
//   model-space transforms (the skeleton's own space, the root bone's parent
//   being the model origin), and 'socketModel' places a socket in it.
// - Two-bone inverse kinematics: 'solveTwoBone' finds the middle and end
//   joints of a limb of two segments whose end must reach a target, with a
//   pole that says which way the middle joint bends; the target is clamped
//   to what the limb can reach and the joint to a smallest interior angle.
//   'aimLimb' applies it to a pose.
// - 'humanoid', a 19-bone mannequin skeleton of a given height with its
//   sockets, from the Drillis and Contini proportions Winter tabulates.
//
// Conventions are geomath's: right-handed, y up, metres, f32. A being faces
// -z at rest: its right hand is +x, its left -x. A positive turn about x of a
// bone that hangs down swings it forward (towards -z).
//
//   bonepose.cone   this overview, scalar helpers
//   skeleton.cone   Bone, Socket, Skeleton, Pose and the forward kinematics
//   ik.cone         solveTwoBone and aimLimb
//   humanoid.cone   the 19-bone skeleton and its bone and socket numbers

