// The 19-bone humanoid, and its bone and socket numbers.
//
// Proportions are fractions of the body's height H, the Drillis and Contini
// (1966) table that Winter's "Biomechanics and Motor Control of Human
// Movement" prints: hip 0.530, knee 0.285, ankle 0.039, shoulder 0.818,
// elbow 0.630, wrist 0.485, chin 0.870, eye 0.936, shoulder width 0.259, hip
// width 0.191, foot length 0.152, hand 0.108. Each bone's offset from its
// parent is the difference of two of those heights. The spine, chest, neck and head
// split the hip-to-shoulder-to-chin run between them (0.070, 0.130, 0.088,
// 0.052; the split is this package's, not the table's).
//
// A being faces -z; its left is -x. The rest pose is standing, the arms 15
// degrees out from the body (an A-pose), hands and feet in line with their
// bones. The root is the pelvis, 0.530 H above the ground point at the
// model's origin.

